course: dictionary
lesson: 4379
title: Habitats
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A habitat is the natural home of an animal or plant. It is the place where a living thing finds the food, water, shelter and space it needs.
explain: «A frog needs two habitats: its tadpoles live in water and breathe with gills, while adult frogs hunt insects on damp land.» The clues show that one frog needs both a pond and damp land, so losing its pond means losing frogs.
explain: «Cutting down forests destroys habitats.» Destroy tells you a habitat can be harmed, like a home. When a habitat is lost, the animals and plants living there lose their food, water, shelter and space.
fact: A habitat is the natural home of an animal or plant.
fact: Forests, deserts, oceans, ponds, grasslands, the Arctic and even your back garden are all habitats.
fact: Animals are adapted to their habitats: a camel to the desert, a polar bear to the Arctic.
fact: Habitat loss, such as cutting down forests, puts animals in danger.
fact: Phrases to know: natural habitat, habitat loss, protect habitats, microhabitat.
exercise: What is a habitat? | A type of rock | A kind of weather | A shop that sells pet food | *The natural home of an animal or plant
exercise: «A camel is adapted to its habitat.» Which habitat is the camel adapted to? | The Arctic | A pond | An ocean | *The desert
exercise: What does habitat loss mean? | *Losing the natural homes that animals need | Building new shops | Animals getting bigger | Learning a new language
test: What is the taiga? | A desert full of sand | A city park | A large ocean | *A huge forest habitat of spruce, pine, fir and larch
test: Why does losing a pond mean losing frogs? | Because frogs eat ponds | Because ponds make frogs taller | Because frogs like to live in cities | *Because the frog's habitat includes water, where its tadpoles live
exam: «An adaptation is a feature that helps a living thing survive in its habitat.» How do adaptations grow? | In one animal's lifetime | Only when a habitat is cut down | In one single week | *Over very many generations
